# Line endings are a property of the REPOSITORY, not of whoever cloned it.
#
# Without this file, each machine's `core.autocrlf` decides what lands in the
# working tree. On Windows that means `true`, which checks out CRLF -- and
# `deno fmt` normalises to LF, so `deno task fmt:check` fails locally on files
# that are perfectly fine in the repo and pass on CI's Linux runner. That
# divergence is invisible until it wastes an hour mid-merge, and it cannot be
# fixed by a config change, because a config change fixes one machine.
#
# `eol=lf` overrides core.autocrlf for checkout as well as commit, so every
# clone gets the same bytes CI sees.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Shell scripts are LF or they do not run under a POSIX shell at all -- a CRLF
# shebang line makes the interpreter path end in a carriage return.
*.sh text eol=lf

# WAT fixtures are text, but they are also DIAGNOSTIC-OFFSET INPUT: parser
# error positions are byte offsets into these files, so their line endings are
# load-bearing rather than cosmetic. Pinned to LF for the same reason as
# everything else, and called out so a future change here is understood to move
# every offset in the test corpus.
*.wat text eol=lf

# Never transform these. No .wasm is tracked today; the project emits them, and
# a line-ending filter applied to a binary would corrupt it silently.
*.wasm binary
*.bin  binary
